      ],
      "source": "https://www.trempealeaucountyhistory.org/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The name Trempealeau comes from a French translation of the Ho Chunk name for a conical island, meaning mountain soaking in the water.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trempealeau%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "An ancient Native American site near Trempealeau features earthwork mounds that may have been a colony of the Mississippian culture from Cahokia.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trempealeau%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Trempealeau Mountain was described in 1824 as a convenient stopping point for the Winnebago, Menominee, and Dakota people traveling the Mississippi River.",
